      "problem": "Solve the inequality $2\\cdot {8}^{2x}<\\frac{1}{3}.$\n\n$x$\n{{field-1}}{{field-2}}",
      "worked_solution": "Isolating the exponential term, we find\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\cdot {8}^{2x} & <\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ {8}^{2x} & <\\frac{1}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, taking the logarithm to base $8$ of both sides and applying the laws of logarithms, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{8}⁡({8}^{2x}) & <{log}_{8}⁡(\\frac{1}{6}) \\\\ 2x & <{log}_{8}⁡({6}^{-1}) \\\\ 2x & <-{log}_{8}⁡(6) \\\\ x & <-\\frac{1}{2}{log}_{8}⁡(6).\\end{aligned}\n$$",
